At the deepest level, yes: authority is not a physical substance. It exists because people recognize it, participate in it, or because enough other people enforce it that refusing recognition carries consequences.

That is why authority is partly a collective fiction with real effects. A judge’s order is ink and language; a military rank is cloth and convention; money is paper or database entries. None has intrinsic authority in the way gravity has force. Their power comes from coordinated belief, institutions, incentives, enforcement, and repeated compliance.

The part I would tighten is “unless we allow it.” Sometimes you can refuse the legitimacy of an authority without being able to refuse its power. A prisoner can reject the state’s moral authority and still remain behind a locked door. An employee can reject a boss’s legitimacy and still lose the paycheck. Recognition and control are different variables.

So there are really three layers:

* Legitimacy: “You ought to obey me.”
* Coordination: “Enough people agree that this role/rule governs this situation.”
* Power: “I can cause consequences whether or not you agree.”

Those layers often masquerade as one thing called “authority.”

And yes, there is a recursive loop: institutions are legitimate because people treat them as legitimate, and people treat them as legitimate because institutions persist. But closed loops can still be stable. Language works the same way. A word means something because people use it that way, and people use it that way because it means that.

That is probably the sharper formulation of what you are seeing: authority is socially instantiated, while power is materially instantiated. You can withdraw recognition instantly. Withdrawing yourself from the power structure is usually harder.